{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/687920-how-do-you-determine-difficulty-for-a-social-roll","id":687920,"title":"How do you determine difficulty for a social roll.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-03T22:16:59-07:00","id":687920,"title":"How do you determine difficulty for a social roll.","content":"What kind of questions or statements require a roll, how do you determine the difficulty of a social roll? In what order do social rolls happen?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8152-xizzicks","name":"Xizzicks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T22:22:26-07:00","id":687944,"title":"","content":"I roll social rolls the same time I would roll any other roll: when there are interesting consequences for success and failure. Generally that means the NPC is resistant to whatever the PCs want.<br /> <br /> I usually have them resist with Resolve, since degrees of success don't matter for social rolls (as per the information on resistance vs contested in the book). I just pick a Resolve I think fits for the NPC in question. A sniveling snitch might have Resolve 1, a 2000 year old Ancient probably has 5 or more.<br /> <br /> I've lately taken to using Forcing Doors for Social Maneuvering as an alternate way to do it, since it's more particular with the difficulty and modifiers. You might look at that for ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T22:40:00-07:00","id":687952,"title":"","content":"What I did today was introduce players to the setting and people around him and he asked my Carthian Gangrel Jackson any questions like who are you, what's going on. etc. He asked who he is. Do I need to roll now? Also do I need to make a character sheet for npcs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8152-xizzicks","name":"Xizzicks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T23:10:17-07:00","id":687967,"title":"","content":"You don't really need a character sheet for NPCs. A lot of the time you can write down a few dice pools for their signature actions, maybe figure out their dot ratings for traits they would use frequently, and eyeball the rest. Like, maybe Jackson has a dice pool of 5 for Rallying the Base, or whatever.<br /> <br /> Like Maina says, you generally want to save dice rolls for interesting actions with at least some tension. In most situations, if you ask somebody who they are, they'll tell you who they are. They might provide a biased view of themselves or leave out some information they don't trust you with, but you'll have something to work with. What would Jackson do if your player failed his roll, just brush him off? That's confusing and not fun. As an example of a situation where asking somebody who they are might warrant a roll, you might ask for a roll if somebody is being cagey, disguising who they are, or brokering a deal that the player might not want to allow to go through if he knew who the buyer was. Even on a failed roll there, he'd tell you something \u2013 just, not the information that will keep you safe or protect your interests.<br /> <br /> In general, it's ideal if a failure feels like it has some kind of active consequence: you piss them off, they fool you, you look weak and people notice. &quot;Knowing&quot; rolls can be an exception, but even then, you want a situation with tension. If you're rolling to understand the meaning of a blood ritual you saw performed, knowing and not knowing makes a tangible difference in how you figure out a response to counteract it.<br /> <br /> As for difficulty? I'd lean more towards applying Composure than Resolve as a resistance trait, on the whole, but I could easily see just eyeballing a dice penalty based on how hard the player character's action sounds. Wheedle some small talk about procedures at work from an Invictus flunky? Sure, small talk is limited in utility, just roll your pool, no penalty. Convince somebody to do you a favor they don't want to be caught doing because it'll be a black mark on their record? -1, maybe -2. Surreptitiously draw incriminating information about a person from their friend? -2, -3. Convince someone to betray their closest and most powerful ally? -5. Maybe more, although formally, any one single modifier isn't meant to exceed a magnitude of 5 dice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-03T23:53:45-07:00","id":687975,"title":"","content":"So for example I explained to my player that he and my character were newly sired, in a concert with loud techno music. I introduced my character Xizzicks a nosferatu scholar and idealist (Mask and dirge respectively.) as being big brow boned with bushy harsh eyebrows light white skin and white eyes with long sharp nails. I explain he was a martial arts instructor (Touchstone Martial arts training, is that acceptable?) to a few people but was also reclusive and studied medicine particularly bone setting and breaking. He also studied martial arts in a training camp as an interesting hobby and had a strong friendship with animals in general. (My skill specialties ok?) He did the same for his character.<br /> <br /> <br /> How do I determine if I go first socially or he does? Is that ST choice? So basically roll on like a try at using nightmare and intimidate to scare the caitiff thugs away from the thin bloods they've been harassing. Not whether a person is truthful?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8152-xizzicks","name":"Xizzicks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-04T03:53:08-07:00","id":688103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/687920-how-do-you-determine-difficulty-for-a-social-roll?p=687944#post687944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I usually have them resist with Resolve, since degrees of success don't matter for social rolls (as per the information on resistance vs contested in the book).</div> </div> </div> </div>Technically the book recommends doing it the other way around: resisted when the degree of successes matters, and contested otherwise. So maybe you misremembered. Not that there is a problem with the way you are doing it, but having social rolls being resisted will result in them almost always succeeding, unless you stack the penalties to get to the really low dice pools (one to three dice).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xizzicks</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/687920-how-do-you-determine-difficulty-for-a-social-roll?p=687975#post687975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do I determine if I go first socially or he does? Is that ST choice? So basically roll on like a try at using nightmare and intimidate to scare the caitiff thugs away from the thin bloods they've been harassing. Not whether a person is truthful?</div> </div> </div> </div>You could always make a contested Presence roll (or some other roll) to see who asserts himself as dominant and can speak first. But in my group that isn't really necessary as conversations flow naturally.<br /> <br /> Most of the social rolls my players make are to see whether someone is lying or is misdirecting them. So at least in my game, we roll to see whether people are truthful. We also have other social rolls, but only when failure on the roll would have relevance to the story, and when it feels to me like it is an action that could fail.<br /> <br /> I should really use more penalties in social rolls, but I usually forget about that because most of my social rolls are contested, and thus don't need penalties to make them interesting. I only use penalties when the players want to do something very outlandish. On the other hand, my players don't ever use equipment bonusses on social rolls. If they did I would use penalties more often.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/304-penta","name":"Penta","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar304_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"617"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-04T08:17:11-07:00","id":688340,"title":"","content":"I usually have my players initiate social rolls. If a player is interacting with an NPC and makes a statement of intent like &quot;I want to see if he's lying to me&quot; or &quot;I want to convince her I'm a doctor&quot; or what have you, then I'll have them roll. Otherwise, we just roleplay it out. As for determining the difficulty of the roll, I just use the Social Maneuvering system. Once they open all of the target's Doors, they achieve their stated intent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-04T08:38:12-07:00","id":688375,"title":"","content":"How does the dice formula work? How do you determine how many dice to roll and how your skills and attributes affect the total?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8152-xizzicks","name":"Xizzicks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-04T10:18:49-07:00","id":688494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xizzicks</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/687920-how-do-you-determine-difficulty-for-a-social-roll?p=688375#post688375\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How does the dice formula work? How do you determine how many dice to roll and how your skills and attributes affect the total?</div> </div> </div> </div>Most rolls are a combination of Attribute + Skill, or occasionally Attribute + Attribute. You determine which traits to roll based on the action. So say you want to threaten someone into doing what you want, you might use Presence + Intimidation. Then you just roll a number of 10-sided dice equal to your total dots in the two traits being used, and any dice that come up 8 or higher are successes. If the roll is contested, the other character would roll an appropriate dice pool (maybe Composure) and who ever gets more successes achieves their intent in the interaction. Or sometimes you might apply a dice penalty (again, the other character's Resolve would be a good one to use in this case), which would subtract from the player's total dice pool.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-04T16:35:42-07:00","id":689002,"title":"","content":"Thanks so much everyone! I think I have a much better understanding of things now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8152-xizzicks","name":"Xizzicks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}